{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg72qo31aQ6V1gysj8xbt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The default is **100**. That's what `postgresql.conf` ships with, and it's also what `initdb` picks when it can get enough shared memory and semaphores. If it can't, it falls back to a lower value, down to a minimum of 10.\n\n- Of those 100 slots, `superuser_reserved_connections` holds back 3 by default. That leaves about 97 for regular users.\n- Managed services often set a different value. For example, RDS and Cloud SQL scale it with instance memory.\n- Changing it requires a server restart.\n- If you need many more connections, use a pooler such as PgBouncer. Raising `max_connections` a lot increases per-connection memory and lock-table overhead.\n\nYou can check your own instance with `SHOW max_connections;`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6758,"cache_read_input_tokens":11227,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6758},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"eb4691d4-b1d9-4272-8e0b-3e5381f44bc9","uuid":"ce472ec6-0847-497b-8d5c-cf12e4ed9703","timestamp":"2026-10-17T02:37:38.278Z","request_id":"req_011Cg72qnpcNWbHKCTEVks6V"}
